{
  "dom_id": "step-q13447",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/50dfc42742418eae8eb25f26fd193b714e06b820f99945ac2b8b759eed3a349e.png)\n\nConsider the figure shown above. Which of the following triplets of points are collinear?\n\nI. $Q,S,T$\n\nII. $M,O,Q$\n\nIII. $N,P,T$",
  "worked_solution": "- $Q,S,T$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/2101dd8ae066aa55d601263de389e013100e476ddbf5220381e4040440b344a9.png)\n\n- $M,O,Q$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/d29ca68ff7123964944eded292ec8ea2c6b16a9fd46e875472e7666f114c8cad.png)\n\n- $N,P,T$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/03db0865e93c7c9a1a2f1c23fd8027602e04299903edb6996b9f0b2150d1d036.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
